WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.360
If starting size alone does not decide, what does?

2
00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.460
Is it silence, or being quieter than a neighbour?

3
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:15.910
In newborn rat muscle, blocking nerve impulses with tetrodotoxin slows elimination.

4
00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:20.963
Chronic electrical stimulation, with some patterns, speeds it up.

5
00:00:21.623 --> 00:00:22.863
So activity sets the pace.

6
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:26.447
But these tests changed every input at once.

7
00:00:27.207 --> 00:00:30.867
They could not say whether a quiet input is lost because of its neighbours.

8
00:00:31.907 --> 00:00:33.307
Two rules were left to separate.

9
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.500
Rule one. A silent synapse is removed.

10
00:00:38.583 --> 00:00:44.643
Rule two. A synapse is removed when it is less active than nearby synapses, if

11
00:00:44.643 --> 00:00:45.763
enough of them are active.

12
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:51.137
To tell them apart, silence part of one junction, and leave the rest working.

13
00:00:52.167 --> 00:00:58.027
Alpha-bungarotoxin, the snake toxin from the imaging, binds acetylcholine receptors

14
00:00:58.027 --> 00:00:59.467
almost irreversibly.

15
00:01:00.347 --> 00:01:01.587
It blocks each one it binds.

16
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.040
Where it sits, the muscle stops responding.

17
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.540
The axon above is untouched.

18
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.060
So quieter here means the muscle responds less.

19
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.800
In a living mouse, put toxin on one small patch of one endplate.

20
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.880
At a second endplate, cover every receptor.

21
00:01:19.983 --> 00:01:24.923
Predict. Over the following weeks, what does each rule say will happen at each

22
00:01:24.923 --> 00:01:25.923
junction?

23
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:34.267
Rule one predicts loss at both junctions.

24
00:01:35.187 --> 00:01:36.187
Both have silent sites.

25
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:42.307
Rule two predicts loss only at a partial block, where a small silent patch sits

26
00:01:42.307 --> 00:01:43.967
beside a larger working area.

27
00:01:44.917 --> 00:01:49.417
In 1994, Rita Balice-Gordon and Lichtman reported the result.

28
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:55.167
At the partial block, the patch lost its receptors, and the nerve terminal withdrew

29
00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:56.167
from it.

30
00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:58.850
At the full block, nothing was eliminated.

31
00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:06.190
At the full block, every site is equally silent.

32
00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:08.890
No site is quieter than its neighbour.

33
00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:11.090
Rule two wins.

34
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.520
the loss stayed inside the patch.

35
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.800
The signal behind it, and how far it travels, were not identified.

36
00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:20.990
The amount of active territory mattered.

37
00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:24.630
A large active area removed a small silent one.

38
00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.050
A small active area could not.

39
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.320
The loss happened where the toxin was put.

40
00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.180
So a local difference in activity directs which sites go.

41
00:02:35.183 --> 00:02:39.603
Lo and Poo built the smallest contest, in cultures from the frog Xenopus.

42
00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:43.367
Two spinal neurons shared one muscle cell.

43
00:02:44.207 --> 00:02:47.307
A burst to one neuron immediately weakened the other's synapse.

44
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.960
Predict. What if both neurons get the burst together?

45
00:02:58.417 --> 00:03:00.317
The suppression was largely absent.

46
00:03:01.337 --> 00:03:03.937
Neither input was left quiet while the other was driven.

47
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.860
This is heterosynaptic depression, a weakening within minutes.

48
00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.580
It fits rule two, but it is not removal of a synapse.

49
00:03:13.583 --> 00:03:17.363
For two whole axons, mice had one of two inputs made weaker.

50
00:03:18.223 --> 00:03:20.323
The more powerful inputs were strongly favoured.

51
00:03:21.383 --> 00:03:23.443
Still, it is a bias, not a verdict.

52
00:03:27.233 --> 00:03:29.233
What carries the message is still open.

53
00:03:30.113 --> 00:03:34.453
In one model, the muscle punishes inactive sites and protects the active one.

54
00:03:35.417 --> 00:03:40.717
Without one form of PKC, elimination in mice is delayed, but it still completes.

55
00:03:41.697 --> 00:03:46.437
Mice lacking BDNF or p75 eliminate synapses on schedule.

56
00:03:47.377 --> 00:03:50.617
So none of these is yet shown to be the indispensable signal.

57
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:54.350
So silence alone does not remove a synapse.

58
00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.410
Being quieter than its neighbours puts it at risk.
